The organization A toJSONString and an Object.assign method.

One method uses the new operator to copy all properties from one object into another. The other method modifies an object and returns a new object.

The following diagram shows the difference.  It’s useful to compare this animation to an animated gif that I made:

This is where all this gets tricky.  When I say “all this gets tricky”, what I mean is that in most of the programming languages I know, it’s not common to use the new operator when copying or modifying existing objects (it’s rare in JavaScript).  Many developers prefer the following:

```js
function copyObject(obj) {
  let copy = {};
  for (let key in obj) {
    copy[key] = obj[key];
  }
  return copy;
}

function modifyObject(obj, setObj) {
  for (let key in setObj) {
    obj[key] = setObj[key];
  }
}

const obj = {
  name: 'David',
  age: 32,
}

modifyObject(obj, {
  profession: 'Developer',
});

console.log(obj)
```

But we can do this too:

```js


function copyObj(obj) {
  let copy = {};
  for (let key in obj) {
    copy[key] = obj[key];
  }
  return copy;
}

function modifyObj(obj, setObj) {
  for (let key in setObj) {
    obj[key] = setObj[key];
  }
}

const obj = {
  name: 'David',
  age: 32,
}

modifyObj(obj, {
  profession: 'Developer',
});

console.log(obj)
```

Note the difference in usage, there’s a difference in error handling and in style.  JavaScript has a very different philosophy than most other languages, this is just one example.  

I know this kind of difference isn’t specific to JavaScript.  

